From 9fae81b3791e440da0bb938f8d150028c32b6b9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timur Ravilov <93673987+t0b1kent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 10:25:49 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 17/19] DIV/IDIV: plain n-bit division after xor edx,edx / CDQ
 / CQO (MACRUNNER_FEX_DIV_PROVEN_HIGH)

Patch 0011 checks for a zero divisor in front of every DIV/IDIV and ends the block there. That
defeats the upstream fold (TryPostRAMerge) that turns `cqo; idiv` into a 64-bit division when the
sign extension is the IR node right before the division, so every IDIV r64 got a runtime check of
the high half (sbfx/eor/cbz, with the LDIV helper behind it) and every DIV r32 built a 64-bit
dividend. xbench on this Mac, ns per iteration: `xor edx,edx; div ecx` 0.861 (MacRunner 1.0.2)
-> 0.986 (series 0001-0015), `cqo; idiv rcx` 0.702 -> 0.947; with MACRUNNER_FEX_DIV_OVERFLOW_DE=1
2.090 and 2.980.

With MACRUNNER_FEX_DIV_PROVEN_HIGH=1 (default off) a DIV r32/r64 that directly follows
`xor edx, edx` / `xor rdx, rdx`, or an IDIV that directly follows CDQ/CQO of the same size, in
the same block, is a plain n-bit UDIV/SDIV with no high half: the high half is known and nothing
can change RAX/RDX in between (a jump target in between starts a new block, which clears the
record). With MACRUNNER_FEX_DIV_OVERFLOW_DE=1 the #DE condition is then only "divisor 0", plus
"INT_MIN / -1" for IDIV, taken as CLZ(x) >> log2(n) without compares or NZCV. Register-cache
values cannot carry the proof: the cache is flushed before every instruction, so the record is
the guest PC right after the idiom.

MacRunnerDivProvenHighUsed counts such translations; a macrunner-fex-div-proven-high line is
printed at 1, 2, 4, ... HB<->FEX oracle, one binary, four arms (no gate / PROVEN / OVERFLOW_DE /
both), 72 219 cases of the earlier corpora plus 133 new idiom cases in product and native mode:
no result changes from adding PROVEN_HIGH, with or without OVERFLOW_DE; the counter fires on
exactly the 120 + 120 idiom cases and the 2 hbcorp cases the disassembler predicts, 0 elsewhere;
with OVERFLOW_DE all 133 idiom cases match (including 10 quotient overflows), without it the
same 10 diverge as before. Negative control (ADD + 1): the same 132 ADD cases turn red.

